The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (par->match->hooks && (par->hook_mask & ~par->match->hooks) != 0) {
```
Reason: This check validates hook mask by bitwise operation without considering that NF_INET_* and NF_ARP_* constants have overlapping numeric values. Therefore, a match registered with NFPROTO_UNSPEC and using NF_INET_* hooks can pass validation for an ARP chain because its allowable hooks happen to match the ARP hook numbers (e.g., NF_ARP_OUT == NF_INET_LOCAL_IN). The match then runs on an ARP hook where its assumptions (e.g., state->in being set for input hooks) are false, causing a NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (par->family == NFPROTO_ARP &&
    par->match->family != NFPROTO_ARP) {
    pr_info_ratelimited("%s_tables: %s match: not valid for this family\n",
                        xt_prefix[par->family], par->match->name);
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
and similarly for target:
```c
if (par->family == NFPROTO_ARP &&
    par->target->family != NFPROTO_ARP) {
    ...
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
Reason: This adds an explicit family check before the hook mask validation. It rejects any match or target that is not specifically registered for NFPROTO_ARP (i.e., with family == NFPROTO_ARP) when the table protocol family is ARP. This prevents NFPROTO_UNSPEC extensions from being used on ARP chains, eliminating the hook mask numerical collision and protecting against incorrect semantics.

